    "body": "Depends on #42061; the first commit is that PR.\n\n## Problem\n\nA terminal response that arrives in pieces with a pause longer than 'ttimeoutlen' between them is typed as keys. E.g. `\"+p` with the OSC 52 clipboard over SSH puts base64 in the buffer when the terminal asks permission before answering: the connection is idle while it asks, and the reply then arrives in bursts about one round trip apart. Same with a small 'ttimeoutlen' in WSL or behind OpenSSH's `ObscureKeystrokeTiming`.\n\n## Solution\n\nWait at least 1 second for the rest of a DCS, OSC or APC string whose content starts with a digit and has no control characters, like the responses to Nvim's queries (OSC 52, OSC 11, XTGETTCAP, DECRQSS). Vim has 'osctimeoutlen' (default 1000, 9.1.1703) for the same problem.\n\n`<M-]>`, `<M-P>` and `<M-_>` send the same bytes as the introducer:\n- `<M-]>` alone, `<M-]><M-]>`, `<M-]>j`, `<M-]><CR>`, `<M-]>5<CR>` (keys 10 ms apart): unchanged, 50-70 ms with the default 'ttimeoutlen'.\n- `<M-]>` then a digit within 'ttimeoutlen': ~1 s instead of ~60 ms, and printable keys typed after it are held until a 1 s pause. With the kitty keyboard protocol, keys do not send these bytes.\n\nThe wait also applies to a response split between the `ESC` and `\\` of its terminator, which `tinput_read_cb()` keeps in the raw buffer. The timer is stopped when nothing is pending, so a leftover timer cannot fire during a later bracketed paste.\n\nNot covered: a pause right after `ESC` or the introducer; APC kitty graphics responses (start with `G`) and XTVERSION (`>|`). When the 1 s runs out, the partial response is still typed as keys (Vim discards it with an error).\n\nAI assistance: this draft was prepared and opened by an AI agent (Claude Code) operating my account. It stays a draft until I have reviewed and tested it myself, per CONTRIBUTING.md.\n",
